 ### Parallel Coordinates Style Interface
 A parallel coordinates style interface that provides an overview of rendering options and transfer function settings, allowing the user to easily explore different parameters and backtrack the visualization history [S1, unofficial]. Tory et al. [89] propose this interface where the user can easily explore different parameters and backtrack the visualization history using the proposed interface [S1, unofficial].
 
+### Topology-controlled Transfer Function UI
+To select a topological zone in a rendered image, a ray is projected through the data from the pixel. The first 'visible' topological zone is selected by evaluating the volume rendering integral for the ray and finding the first sample with opacity above a threshold [S5]. The fully augmented contour tree is used to determine the topological zone to which the selected sample (from ray) or pixel (from slice) belongs [S5]. The contour tree is displayed in two representations: a TOPORRERY view (3D radial layout where height corresponds to function value) and a traditional 2D view (y-coordinate corresponds to function value) [S5]. A vertical transfer function editor is included next to the contour tree interface. The y-coordinates of the contour tree interface and the transfer function editor are linked to provide contextual topological information [S5]. For volume-rendered data with multiple layers, ray casting visibility is not straightforward; a slice view is provided as an alternative where the user selects the topological zone's intersection with that slice [S5].
+
 ### WYSIWYG Semantic Representation
-An adaptation of the WYSIWYG (What You See Is What You Get) concept to volumetric exploration, implementing a high-level semantic representation with drawing tools [S5, unofficial]. Guo, Mao and Yuan (2011) adapted the concept WYSIWYG to volumetric exploration, implementing a high-level semantic representation that provides drawing tools to the user [S5, unofficial].
